Android code for Liturgia hodin. (c) Richard Kralovic.

Java code and android io glue (c) Richard Kralovic, GPLv3 license. Some
parts of this code were inspired by com.bolutions.webserver (under GPLv3).

Copyright on texts is owned by Spolok sv. Vojtecha, which gave permission to
use them in the project Liturgia hodin.

To build:

$ download-and-build-assets
$ gradle assembleRelease

E-ink interface:

On first launch, choose Normal display or E-ink display in the black-on-white
chooser, before the changelog. Upgrades also show it once if no explicit mode
was previously saved; existing choices (including Normal display) are retained.
Later upgrades do not ask again. Change the choice in Settings > Device >
E-ink mode. This device-local preference survives restarts and applies to the
reader, native settings, language selection, notifications settings and help.
Switching modes retains the current prayer, reading position and saved prayer
options. Prayer pages retain the normal CSS, including night mode and custom
colors. The calendar remains in the prayer page.

An appearance change rebuilds covered screens before rebuilding Device Settings,
so returning through the settings hierarchy does not reveal the old toolbar
colors. An immediate toolbar Up or system Back waits for that handoff and is
then applied to the replacement screen. Return transitions are suppressed for
the affected screens in both directions; ordinary navigation keeps its behavior.

E-ink mode uses black text on white and outlined controls in native menus, with
no activity/drawer transitions. When enabled, the bottom page buttons,
existing in-text page actions, volume keys (when enabled) and hardware Page Up /
Page Down keys jump without animated scrolling, with overlap between pages.
The drawer opens with the toolbar menu button instead of an animated edge swipe.
Text selection, links, zoom and touch scrolling remain available.

Reading navigation:

Settings > Display > Reading navigation offers scrolling only, navigation
arrows in the text, up/down buttons below the page, tapping the top/bottom
thirds, or tapping the left/right thirds of the reading area.
Scrolling works in all five modes.
The choice applies to both standard and e-ink interfaces; changing
appearance does not change navigation. Arrow position and down-only preferences
remain available when arrows are selected. Bottom buttons also appear in help
and other HTML dialogs.

Existing arrow settings are retained. On upgrade, e-ink users without arrows
keep their bottom buttons; other users keep ordinary scrolling. Arrow mode uses
the existing URL option, so links and web settings can still enable or disable
arrows. Disabling arrows returns to scrolling or the saved button/tap choice.

Tap navigation applies to generated prayer/calendar pages and HTML help dialogs.
Tap the top third of the visible WebView to page up or the bottom third to page
down; in left/right mode, tap the left third to page up or the right third to
page down. Both modes page immediately after HTML handlers have run, without
waiting for a second tap.
A single tap in the middle third does nothing; two unused taps there toggle
reader fullscreen. Both taps must start and end in that middle third (a horizontal
band in top/bottom mode, a vertical band in left/right mode).
HTML controls keep their actions,
including links, footnotes, calendar fields and help buttons. Selection, long
press, drag, pinch, keyboard and accessibility activation do not turn pages.
External pages and unaudited static HTML retain ordinary browsing behavior.

HTML interaction contract (android/assets/tap-navigation.js): standard controls
and their descendants are reserved automatically, as are inline click handlers.
Custom interactive elements should have data-breviar-interactive, or their
handler must call BreviarTaps.claim(event) synchronously before taking action,
including before scheduling asynchronous work. This does not preventDefault or
stopPropagation, so existing browser behavior is preserved. Delegated handlers
must claim the actual event; simply having a listener does not reserve a tap.
New custom controls must follow this convention. The Android-only wrapper loads
the shared script; inline help embeds that same asset. Missing/uncertain reports
never page. Document tokens and gesture IDs reject late/duplicate reports, and
native touch, scroll, selection, lifecycle and menu changes cancel pending work.

Prayer rendering, CSS, XML option encoding and the web/iOS interfaces retain
their existing behavior. No manufacturer SDK is required; waveform selection,
full-screen refresh and ghosting mitigation remain device/system features.

Verification:

$ make -C testing settings  # CGI settings integration checks
$ make -C testing taps  # Real Chromium mouse/touch events (requires websocket-client)
$ gradle testDebugUnitTest assembleDebug  # Android lifecycle and navigation tests

On an e-reader, check settings > device > e-ink, Back through XML submenus,
page buttons/volume keys, tap thirds, calendar links/fields, selection and pinch,
double-tap fullscreen, night mode, search, TTS controls,
and switching back. Also switch modes and immediately press toolbar Up or system
Back: no old-color toolbar should flash on either the parent settings or reader.
Check a cold launch in each mode and confirm the device's
chosen refresh settings are comfortable. Automated checks cannot assess panel
ghosting or device-specific refresh behavior.

Check the startup chooser on a clean install and an upgrade without a saved
appearance. Both choices must be readable, precede the changelog, and persist
after restarting. An existing explicit mode must skip the chooser. On a BOOX
reader, also check the normal drawer with the device's app optimization enabled;
its explicit colors match the original phone palette, but vendor display
processing is outside the scope of the automated tests.
